{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790463316,"nonce":"gXXqx5Jq31AJ4xjPDvfGRuakdCM=","title":"Python: calling a field that holds a callable is refused at lowering","message":"A call `self.f(t)` where `f` is an instance field holding a function or a callable instance (not a method of the class) fails to lower with \"method `f` of Holder, which defines none is not supported yet\". CPython calls the field value. Binding it first (`f = self.f; f(t)`) works.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    class Line(object):\n        def __init__(self, a): self.a = a\n        def __call__(self, t): return self.a * t\n    class Holder(object):\n        def __init__(self, f): self.f = f\n        def at(self, t): return self.f(t)\n    print(Holder(Line(2)).at(3))\n\nExpected 6.","files":null}]}